1	//! # x87: the floating-point unit Reva's float library drives
2	//!
3	//! `lib/math/floats` (and CoSy, which keeps its floats as 80-bit values)
4	//! writes x87 instructions directly as bytes: `f+` is `inline{ de c1 }`,
5	//! `f@` is `fld tbyte [eax]`.  This module gives the vx86 VM that unit:
6	//! an eight-register stack with tags, `TOP`, the control word (rounding
7	//! mode) and the status word (condition codes `C0`–`C3`, sticky exception
8	//! flags), with every exception masked, as Reva left them.
9	//!
10	//! **Precision.** Registers hold `f64`, not 80-bit extended values.  Loads
11	//! and stores of the 80-bit memory format (`fld`/`fstp tbyte`) are exact
12	//! in the direction f64 → f80 and round to nearest-even in the direction
13	//! f80 → f64; 64-bit integers convert with f64 rounding; arithmetic rounds
14	//! to f64 whatever the precision-control field says, and the rounding
15	//! control field applies to integer conversion (`fist`, `frndint`) only.
16	//! Results can therefore differ from a real x87 in the last bits of the
17	//! extended format; for values that fit a double they are the IEEE double
18	//! results.  `fsin`, `fyl2x` and friends use Rust's `f64` functions.
19	//!
20	//! Not provided (they fault as unsupported instructions): `fldenv`,
21	//! `fstenv`, `fsave`, `frstor`, `fbld`, `fbstp`.
22	
