1://! # reva-desktop: the Reva REPL in a native window
2://!
3://! The window shows the same page as the browser build (`components/web/crates/reva-web/www`)
4://! and runs the same WebAssembly VM in a Web Worker.  What the desktop adds
5://! is a host for that page (`host.js`, served in place of the browser's):
6://!
7://! * **Files on disk.**  The page keeps files through a Web Storage object.
8://!   Here that object is backed by a folder: every file under it is handed
9://!   to the VM at start (VM path `/x/y` = `<root>/x/y`), and each file the
10://!   VM writes or deletes is written back through the commands below.
11://!   The root is `~/Reva`, or the package folder.
12://! * **Packages.**  `--package DIR` opens a folder with a `reva-package.json`
13://!   manifest (`name`, `start`: files to `include` at start, and again
14://!   after Forth's `restart`).  The manifest may also brand the window:
15://!   `title`, `icon` (a PNG in the package) and `id` (the Linux app id
16://!   that matches a `.desktop` entry; see [`Branding`]).  CoSy is one,
17://!   kept in its own repository; this app knows nothing about CoSy.
18://! * **Batch use.**  `--eval TEXT` feeds lines after start and
19://!   `--print-output` copies the VM's output to stdout, so the app can be
20://!   smoke-tested without a person (`xvfb-run reva-desktop --eval ...`).
21://!   `bye` closes the window with Forth's exit code.
22://!
23://! * **More windows.**  `open_window` opens a page of the app (`canvas`:
24://!   turtle graphics) in a second native window; the pages talk through
25://!   the events `reva-ui-down` (main → window) and `reva-ui-up` (window →
26://!   main, including `closed` when it is closed).  See www/ui.js.
27://!
28://! Why the VM stays in the page and not in this process: one VM build for
29://! the browser and the desktop, so both behave the same.  The commands
30://! below are the whole native surface, and every path they take is checked
31://! to stay under the root.
