The binary is self-contained: `reva.f` and `lib/` are compiled in.

## 1. The terminal REPL

Run `reva` with no arguments. It prints the banner and a prompt:

```text
reva-fork 2026.1102.0 portable
ok>
```

Type these lines one at a time (the prompt is left out below):


When standard input is a terminal, `reva` starts as Reva always did: it
prints the banner (`reva-fork 2026.1102.0 portable`) and an `ok>` prompt before
each line. Piped or redirected input gets neither, so batch output is just
what the program prints.
const reva = await Reva.load(wasm);
let failed = 0;
const check = (what, got, want) => {
  const ok = JSON.stringify(got) === JSON.stringify(want);
  if (!ok) failed++;
  console.log(`${ok ? "ok  " : "FAIL"} ${what}: ${JSON.stringify(got)}`);
};

// The boot ends as Reva's saved executable started: banner, then a prompt.
const BANNER = "reva-fork 2026.1102.0 portable\n\nok> ";
check("boot status", reva.status, NEED_INPUT);
// The footer's facts come from the module (reva_vm::version), as `reva -V`.
const BUILD_KEYS = ["name", "version", "copyright", "license", "repository", "build_host", "build_commit", "build_time"];
const build = reva.build;
check("build facts", [Object.keys(build), build.name, build.version], [BUILD_KEYS, "reva-fork", BANNER.split(" ")[1]]);
reva.feed("2 3 + . : sq dup * ; 7 sq .\n");
check("arith", await (async () => { let o = ""; await reva.run((s) => (o += s)); return o; })(), BANNER + "5 49 \nok> ");

reva.feed(": forever forever ; forever\n");
check("infinite loop yields", reva.step(5000), YIELDED);
check("still yielding", reva.step(5000), YIELDED);
